<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harar is a walled city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, a centre of Islamic learning and trade. It became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> and the base for campaigns against the Christian Ethiopian state. Its walls were built after the Adal wars, and the city remained a leading centre of the region after the sultanate's decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harar.jpg" alt="Harar" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rod Waddington from Kergunyah, Australia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_Gate,_Harar_Jugol_(14464345823).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Harar stands on a hilltop in the eastern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, about 500 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> and 1,885 metres above sea level. Its people called it Harar-Gey, or simply Gey, meaning the city. For centuries it was a major commercial centre linked by trade routes with the rest of Ethiopia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The old walled city, Harar Jugol, covers about 60 hectares and is divided into five quarters. It has five ancient gates: Assum <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, Argobba Bari, Suqutat Bari, Badro Bari and Asmadin Bari.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>It is unclear when Harar was founded, and proposed dates run from a Qurayshi settlement in the late 7th century to a local creation in the 10th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> had gained a foothold on the plateau by the 10th and 11th centuries through trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>, and by the 13th century it was the predominant religion. A history of the city records that the saint Abadir Umar ar-Rida and other religious leaders settled on the plateau about 1216.</p><p>In 1520 Harar became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> under Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-muhammad">Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad</a>. Imam Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a>, called Gragn, killed the sultan in 1525 and made Harar the centre of campaigns that ravaged most of Christian Ethiopia. After his defeat in 1543, his widow Bati del Wambara renewed the war, and in 1551 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-ibn-mujahid">Nur ibn Mujahid</a> married her and became ruler.</p><p>In the 17th century Ali ibn Da'ud founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Harar, a dynasty that ruled for the next two centuries. His successor Abdullah improved relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> by marrying five of his sons to Oromo women, and the emirate extended its economic influence from Shewa to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a> and began to mint its own coins. In 1887 the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> conquered Harar and appointed his nephew Ras Makonnen as governor. Makonnen had the main mosque torn down and replaced it with an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Harar is the capital of the Harari Region, and Harar Jugol was listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2006. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> describes it as considered the fourth holiest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, with 82 mosques, three of which date from the 10th century, and 102 shrines. Harari houses, called gey gar, form a distinct type whose layout dates back to 1754. They stand in walled compounds arranged around a courtyard. The French poet Arthur Rimbaud settled in Harar as a merchant between 1880 and 1891, and his house is one of its landmarks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sabr ad-Din III reconstituted the Walashma state in the eastern lowlands of Adal, with its seat at Dakkar near <b>Harar</b> and the port of Zeila as its commercial outlet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Haqq ad-Din II fought the emperor ceaselessly from the <b>Harar</b> plateau and Zeila, in more than twenty battles to 1370, and died in battle in 1376; Sa'ad ad-Din II waged hit-and-run raids and struck chiefs loyal to the emperor at Zalan and Hadeya, until the imperial army defeated…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-ifat">Sultanate of Ifat · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Adal leadership moved to the Imamate of Aussa, <b>Harar</b> was left to local governors.</blockquote><a href="/state/harar-emirate">Harar Emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi and decades of war, the Adal Sultanate was weakened by Oromo raids on <b>Harar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/imamate-of-aussa">Imamate of Aussa</a></li><li><blockquote>Shewa controlled trade routes from the Gibe region and <b>Harar</b> to the coast at Tadjoura and Zeila.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-shewa">Kingdom of Shewa</a></li><li><blockquote>Tadjoura's wealth came from caravans bringing slaves, ivory, coffee and salt from Shewa and <b>Harar</b> to the coast, and from its role as a stopping place for Arab traders.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-tadjoura">Sultanate of Tadjoura</a></li></ul></section>
